711 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1033 332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1034 0.9795434122769167 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050711 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "608cf86b-83ea-5cc3-a6a8-222e7fa44e21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096417 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096417 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096417 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096418 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096418 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096418 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096419 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3636" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096419 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096419 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186240845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096617 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1759218