17592186329890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186514464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536061 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536065 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536069 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536071 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536074 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536075 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536077 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536087 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536090 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186536098 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186571432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186536099 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096619 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1045 "The Product Rule for Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186096889 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0693eaa1-2cbe-58a3-aa5d-c539aa74793e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097425 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097425 #edb/ref 1061 12327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097425 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef3171cb-fb23-5d4d-8819-73da9533de18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097425 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097426 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097426 #edb/ref 1061 12328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097426 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e27ef05d-bc90-5cce-9885-8f21279044e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097426 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097427 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097427 #edb/ref 1061 12329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cbd3b19-1c1f-519e-b232-315484ae9234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097428 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097428 #edb/ref 1061 12330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097428 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a3a2bef-3f64-510c-8bf8-25d81169d680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097428 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097429 #edb/ref 1061 12331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097429 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "138782c6-ad9e-5b08-b3dd-375476961e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097429 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{′} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1065 2622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "958f67f2-8e00-5818-8af7-9f88561ef84c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097818 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097819 #edb/ref 1064 "The product rule can also be written using *Leibniz notation*:\n\nIf $y = u(x) \\cdot v(x),$ then \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot  v + u \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097819 #edb/ref 1065 2623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097819 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3c6d5393-f91c-57e5-999c-92eedf550f2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097819 #edb/ref 1067 "Leibniz Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285251 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}sec⁡x+{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285251 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285251 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "329a0351-2458-5dc2-afb3-fb83d076e70a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285366 #edb/ref 1080 "{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285366 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285366 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d41ba8e-fd4a-5c5b-8e2c-3eb30e482c53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285621 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}{csc}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b2ff268-76d1-583f-9abe-62b917b9c7eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286129 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+tan⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286129 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286129 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93783351-25ff-5c87-8ae9-958c98c327e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286945 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286945 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186286945 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb7d1570-7a65-5731-b765-bf52675c003d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186286945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287478 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a44b9e2e-ea9e-5094-bc4f-bc781e7df5d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate ${e}^{x}tan⁡x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a541e9ea-7fd8-5568-9e5c-f9ef03f5eaf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287942 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}tan⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (tan⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(tan⁡x)+({e}^{x})({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327088 #edb/ref 1080 "3e+5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327088 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327088 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "122dcace-6bca-59bb-b96f-a96026e9a833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327286 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29771cef-6628-575d-b6d2-039fa89b2a35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327368 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{2}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327368 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327368 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3101f4e9-df22-5461-be7e-f6674a765dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328648 #edb/ref 1080 "19{e}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328648 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328648 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c3e4fd4c-0122-574c-8317-faf21984d650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328905 #edb/ref 1080 "4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328905 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328905 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e154d586-179b-57cc-acf3-bc95bc04c67c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186328648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329378 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "745d0171-7216-5576-96bb-4a33b2c2f9b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{3}-1){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1066779d-ebe1-53a9-ad1f-5e9c5ee1f7e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329892 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1)|}_{x=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}({2}^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =19{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $19{e}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530622 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530581 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00f9082d-0d86-5f9e-9f49-460c209ee39d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530581 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{4} + 1$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{4} + 1)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{4} + 1)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(16 x^{3})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{4} + 1)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =4 x^{4} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 x^{4} + 16 x^{3} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530582 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530643 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530582 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530582 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(4 x^{3} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530582 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "014ac4bb-7a72-5e35-b106-ef044de05a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530582 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{3} + 4$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{3} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{3} + 4)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{3} + 4)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(12 x^{2})(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 x^{3} + 4)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =4 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530588 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530585 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08394ee5-3a76-579f-a293-88b55155a354" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530585 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{4} + x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{4} + x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)+(4 x^{4} + x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)' \\\\ & =(16 x^{3} + 1)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2)+(4 x^{4} + x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =4 x^{4} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 x^{4} + 16 x^{3} \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 32 x^{3} + x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + x + \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530605 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530618 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530605 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530605 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(4 - x^{2})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530605 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "510fd763-1a95-5bb1-8519-840cc0dd1707" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530605 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 - x^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 - x^{2})(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 - x^{2})'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 - x^{2})\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(- 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(4 - x^{2})(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - x^{2} - 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530596 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530609 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58b0ec5d-8f12-5457-8e04-8865bf9e217c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530609 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- x^{2} + 4 x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- x^{2} + 4 x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)+(- x^{2} + 4 x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)' \\\\ & =(4 - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 3)+(- x^{2} + 4 x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - x^{2} + 4 x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 10 x + 4 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530611 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530616 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "726118ef-5aa2-561b-b07d-8ea17d1719c5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530616 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=4 x^{3} + 4 x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(4 x^{3} + 4 x)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(4 x^{3} + 4 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)' \\\\ & =(12 x^{2} + 4)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(4 x^{3} + 4 x)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =4 x^{3} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 12 x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 48 x^{2} + 4 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 16\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530612 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530624 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94a83bf3-2f19-5bd8-9519-a3fb2385c4db" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530624 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=3 x^{2} - 2 x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(3 x^{2} - 2 x)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)+(3 x^{2} - 2 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)' \\\\ & =(6 x - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 1)+(3 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 6 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 6 x - 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530625 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530627 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a77347a8-80b9-5bce-883a-cdfb18cc9a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530627 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)' \\\\ & =(- 4 x - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)} + 4)+(- 2 x^{2} - 2 x)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x^{2} - 2 x \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 4 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 18 x - 2 \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530628 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530613 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530628 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530628 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(3 x^{2} - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530628 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9ff3c61-ade3-53cb-bb8e-71f5650e1f69" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530628 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=3 x^{2} - 2$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((3 x^{2} - 2)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(3 x^{2} - 2)'\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(3 x^{2} - 2)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(6 x)(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(3 x^{2} - 2)(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ & =3 x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 6 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530591 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530641 #edb/ref 1071 "Differentiate $(- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0089896-05d5-55f1-8924-df3c13fcf733" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530641 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=- 2 x^{2} - 2$ and $v(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)}))^{'} & =u'(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot v'(x) \\\\ & =(- 2 x^{2} - 2)'\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(- 2 x^{2} - 2)\\cdot (\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})' \\\\ & =(- 4 x)(\\tan{\\left(x \\right)})+(- 2 x^{2} - 2)(\\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} + 1) \\\\ & =- 2 x^{2} \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x^{2} - 4 x \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 \\tan^{2}{\\left(x \\right)} - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530651 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096617.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186530651 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "d524aae1-2585-5066-9560-5351df946ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536066 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536061 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3 x^{2} + x - 1)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e^{2}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "025970ae-87f8-5c12-8836-70b62439e2c5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536061 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3 x^{2} + x - 1$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=6 x + 1,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(3 x^{2} + x - 1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (6 x + 1) \\\\ &=\\frac{6 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x^{2} + x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + x - 1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e^{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e^{2}} &=(3 (e^{2})^{2} + (e^{2}) - 1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{(e^{2})})+(\\ln{\\left((e^{2}) \\right)})\\cdot (6 (e^{2}) + 1) \\\\ &=(-1 + e^{2} + 3 e^{4})\\cdot (e^{-2})+(2)\\cdot (1 + 6 e^{2}) \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{e^{2}} + 3 + 15 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- \\frac{1}{e^{2}} + 3 + 15 e^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536079 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536065 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(4 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11508a06-1238-59d6-9f30-ce054ca95db7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536065 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=4 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=4 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(4 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (4 e^{x}) \\\\ &=4 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(4 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (4 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(4)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (4) \\\\ &=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536092 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536069 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33ab5b35-dd61-5afa-9e50-76ac2177002e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536069 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - x - 1$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 1,\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - x - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 1) \\\\ &=- x^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=(- (\\frac{\\pi}{4})^{2} - (\\frac{\\pi}{4}) - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})+(\\sin{\\left((\\frac{\\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 (\\frac{\\pi}{4}) - 1) \\\\ &=(-1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4} - \\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16})\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{\\pi}{2} - 1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(- 12 \\pi - 32 - \\pi^{2}\\right)}{32}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536076 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536071 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- e^{x})\\cdot (x + 3)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3897814a-55c8-55e3-8f23-2d1a17edc389" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536071 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 3)\\cdot (- e^{x}) \\\\ &=- \\left(x + 4\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 3)\\cdot (- e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-1)\\cdot (1)+(3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ &=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536083 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536074 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 4 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3be093e9-a88a-5893-84f7-f2f4d4e8fb03" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536074 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 4 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 4 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 4 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (- 4 e^{x}) \\\\ &=- 4 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- 4 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (- 4 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-4)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ &=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536075 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536082 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536075 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536075 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - 2 x + 2)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e^{2}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cc06495-0e47-5717-9700-118ad0cf690b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536075 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - 2 x + 2$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 2,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - 2 x + 2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 2) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + x^{2} + 2 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 x - 2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e^{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e^{2}} &=(- (e^{2})^{2} - 2 (e^{2}) + 2)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{(e^{2})})+(\\ln{\\left((e^{2}) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 (e^{2}) - 2) \\\\ &=(- e^{4} - 2 e^{2} + 2)\\cdot (e^{-2})+(2)\\cdot (- 2 e^{2} - 2) \\\\ &=- 5 e^{2} - 6 + \\frac{2}{e^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- 5 e^{2} - 6 + \\frac{2}{e^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536070 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536077 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45e8eff6-7be8-5e2f-8927-eac30ddd0bc6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536077 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4$ and $v(x)=\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=3 - 6 x,\\quad v'(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 3 x^{2} + 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})+(\\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (3 - 6 x) \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 x^{2} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x^{2} - 3 x \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 x + 4}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=e} &=(- 3 (e)^{2} + 3 (e) - 4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{(e)})+(\\ln{\\left((e) \\right)})\\cdot (3 - 6 (e)) \\\\ &=(- 3 e^{2} - 4 + 3 e)\\cdot (e^{-1})+(1)\\cdot (3 - 6 e) \\\\ &=- 9 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- 9 e - \\frac{4}{e} + 6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536067 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536087 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89a63571-dce9-5ef0-8359-7d12fffc0179" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536087 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{2} - 3 x - 4$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 2 x - 3,\\quad v'(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- x^{2} - 3 x - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 x - 3) \\\\ &=x^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 x \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}} &=(- (\\frac{3 \\pi}{4})^{2} - 3 (\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) - 4)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) \\right)})+(\\cos{\\left((\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) \\right)})\\cdot (- 2 (\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}) - 3) \\\\ &=(- \\frac{9 \\pi}{4} - \\frac{9 \\pi^{2}}{16} - 4)\\cdot (- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2} - 3) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(9 \\pi^{2} + 112 + 60 \\pi\\right)}{32}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536089 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536090 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 2)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92e053cc-163c-5fb4-9084-c721d7a398e6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536090 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=3 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(3 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 2)\\cdot (3 e^{x}) \\\\ &=3 \\left(x + 3\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(3 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 2)\\cdot (3 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(3)\\cdot (1)+(2)\\cdot (3) \\\\ &=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536080 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536098 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- 3 e^{x})\\cdot (x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$.\n\nSlope $=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fad32388-6506-515e-9fd4-68888763cf2f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536098 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- 3 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x + 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 3 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(- 3 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x + 1)\\cdot (- 3 e^{x}) \\\\ &=- 3 \\left(x + 2\\right) e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=(- 3 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1)+((0) + 1)\\cdot (- 3 e^{(0)}) \\\\ &=(-3)\\cdot (1)+(1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ &=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536099 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186096619.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536099 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "45cc5958-d828-5815-b6f0-943020caeb66" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186571014 #edb/ref 1080 "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571014 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571014 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4fbb081d-8fbf-5577-b042-226064e10be0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571145 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571145 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186571145 #edb/ref 1083 #u